---
/** Track validity guide — how GlideComp checks that a submitted tracklog is
 * plausibly the pilot's flight of that task, which checks withhold a track
 * from scoring, and how a scorekeeper overrules the automatic verdict.
 * Sibling of the GAP, open-distance and data-cleaning pages.
 *
 * The thresholds quoted here are the constants in engine track-quality.ts —
 * keep them in step. */
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
import BackLink from "../../components/BackLink.astro";
import SpecRef from "../../components/SpecRef.astro";

const S7A_PDF =
  "https://www.fai.org/sites/default/files/civl/documents/sporting_code_s7_a_-_cross_country_2025.pdf";
---

<Base
  title="GlideComp - How Track Validity Checks Work"
  description="How GlideComp checks that a tracklog is really that pilot's flight of that task — which checks withhold a track from scoring, which only flag it, and how a scorekeeper overrules the verdict."
>
  <div class="min-h-screen bg-background text-foreground p-6">
    <div class="max-w-2xl mx-auto">
      <header class="mb-10">
        <BackLink href="/scoring" label="Scoring" />
        <h1 class="text-3xl font-bold flex items-center gap-3">
          <img src="/icon.svg" alt="GlideComp logo" class="w-8 h-8" />
          How Track Validity Checks Work
        </h1>
        <p class="text-muted-foreground mt-1">
          Checking that a tracklog is really your flight of this task
        </p>
      </header>

      {/* Table of Contents */}
      <nav class="mb-10 p-4 rounded-lg border border-border bg-card">
        <h2 class="text-sm font-semibold uppercase tracking-wide text-muted-foreground mb-2">
          Contents
        </h2>
        <ul class="space-y-1 text-sm">
          <li><a href="#why" class="hover:underline">Why Tracks Get Checked</a></li>
          <li><a href="#withheld" class="hover:underline">The Two Checks That Withhold a Track</a></li>
          <li><a href="#flags" class="hover:underline">The Three Flags That Never Change a Score</a></li>
          <li><a href="#score" class="hover:underline">What It Does to the Score</a></li>
          <li><a href="#override" class="hover:underline">The Scorekeeper Decides</a></li>
          <li><a href="#calibration" class="hover:underline">Where the Thresholds Come From</a></li>
          <li><a href="#disclosure" class="hover:underline">Nothing Happens Silently</a></li>
        </ul>
      </nav>

      {/* Why */}
      <section id="why" class="mb-14">
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold mb-4">Why Tracks Get Checked</h2>
        <div class="space-y-4 text-sm leading-relaxed">
          <p class="text-muted-foreground">
            Sometimes the wrong file gets uploaded — a flight from another day, or another
            site. It is an easy mistake and a hard one to spot: the file is valid IGC, it
            opens, and it looks like flying.
          </p>
          <p class="text-muted-foreground">
            Scoring cannot catch it. Tasks often run across UTC midnight, so GlideComp resolves
            start gates and deadlines <em>relative to the flight being scored</em> rather than
            to a fixed date. A tracklog from another day therefore carries the whole task onto
            its own day and scores as though nothing were wrong.
          </p>
          <p class="text-muted-foreground">
            <a href={S7A_PDF} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="underline hover:text-foreground">FAI S7A §4.4.2</a>
            makes this the software's job: the points used to verify a flight must have
            "occurred at reasonable times (e.g. on the day in question)".
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>

      {/* Withheld */}
      <section id="withheld" class="mb-14">
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold mb-4">The Two Checks That Withhold a Track</h2>
        <div class="space-y-4 text-sm leading-relaxed">
          <p class="text-muted-foreground">
            Both ask one question — <strong class="text-foreground">is this file this
            flight?</strong> — with margins no honest entry can reach.
          </p>

          <div class="p-4 rounded-lg border border-border bg-card">
            <h3 class="text-base font-semibold mb-1">Wrong day</h3>
            <p class="text-muted-foreground">
              Fixes are compared against the task's calendar day in the competition's time
              zone, widened four hours at each end. It fails only if
              <strong class="text-foreground">every</strong> fix falls outside, so an overnight
              recorder, a dawn launch or a flight across local midnight all pass. Being out by
              up to a day is flagged, not withheld — a recorder set one day wrong is a common
              fault on a genuine flight.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="p-4 rounded-lg border border-border bg-card">
            <h3 class="text-base font-semibold mb-1">Wrong location</h3>
            <p class="text-muted-foreground">
              No fix comes within <strong class="text-foreground">100 km</strong> of any
              turnpoint. A long course does not make this stricter: turnpoints are spread along
              it, and anyone who launched starts metres from one.
            </p>
          </div>

          <p class="text-muted-foreground">
            A track failing either is kept out of scoring and out of the field analysis, where
            its clock and its air would distort the day's statistics for everyone else.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>

      {/* Flags */}
      <section id="flags" class="mb-14">
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold mb-4">The Three Flags That Never Change a Score</h2>
        <div class="space-y-4 text-sm leading-relaxed">
          <p class="text-muted-foreground">
            These describe the flying, not the file. Each one fires on legitimate flights too,
            so they are shown for a human to judge and
            <strong class="text-foreground">never affect a score</strong>.
          </p>
          <ul class="space-y-3 text-muted-foreground">
            <li>
              <strong class="text-foreground">Never left the take-off cylinder.</strong> What a
              pilot who launches and sinks straight back looks like — correctly scored the
              minimum distance — and also what a recorder left running on launch looks like.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong class="text-foreground">No sign of flight.</strong> No sustained climb
              (1.5 m/s for two minutes) and no sustained glide (90 seconds descending at
              0.8 m/s, never below 4 m/s over the ground, roughly straight). This is what
              catches a vario recording the drive up to launch — speed alone cannot, since a
              retrieve vehicle outpaces a paraglider.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong class="text-foreground">Implausible speed.</strong> Ground speed held for
              a full minute above about 119 km/h for paragliders or 162 km/h for hang gliders,
              tailwind included. The minute-long window keeps GPS jitter out. It is looking for
              something that is not a glider at all.
            </li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </section>

      {/* Score */}
      <section id="score" class="mb-14">
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold mb-4">What It Does to the Score</h2>
        <div class="space-y-4 text-sm leading-relaxed">
          <p class="text-muted-foreground">
            A pilot who launches and lands short is scored the
            <a href="/scoring/gap#minimum-distance" class="underline hover:text-foreground">minimum distance</a>,
            about 5 km. That is unchanged — a short, unlucky flight still earns it.
          </p>
          <p class="text-muted-foreground">
            A withheld tracklog is different: it is not evidence the pilot flew this task. Those
            pilots keep their place in the results with
            <strong class="text-foreground">zero points</strong> and the reasons beside their
            name. They are never dropped from the standings.
          </p>
          <p class="text-muted-foreground">
            Nobody else's score moves. The pilot is left out of the launch-validity count
            (<SpecRef section="9.1" label="S7F §9.1" />) rather than counted as having flown, so
            a wrong verdict can only ever cost that one pilot.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>

      {/* Override */}
      <section id="override" class="mb-14">
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold mb-4">The Scorekeeper Decides</h2>
        <div class="space-y-4 text-sm leading-relaxed">
          <p class="text-muted-foreground">
            Under
            <a href={S7A_PDF} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="underline hover:text-foreground">S7A §4.4.6</a>
            rejecting a track log is the <strong class="text-foreground">organiser's</strong>
            call, not the software's. A scorekeeper can accept a flagged tracklog in one action:
            it is then scored normally, its findings stay visible, and the decision goes into the
            competition's public audit log.
          </p>
          <p class="text-muted-foreground">
            If your track was withheld, the fix is usually to upload the right file. If it
            <em>is</em> the right file, ask the scorekeeper.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>

      {/* Calibration */}
      <section id="calibration" class="mb-14">
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold mb-4">Where the Thresholds Come From</h2>
        <div class="space-y-4 text-sm leading-relaxed">
          <p class="text-muted-foreground">
            Wrongly zeroing a pilot is worse than missing a bad file, so the thresholds come
            from real competition data.
          </p>
          <p class="text-muted-foreground">
            Across <strong class="text-foreground">4,762</strong> archived tracks, the typical
            flight passes within 70 m of a turnpoint, and the furthest any honest track stayed
            from every turnpoint was 45 km. The 100 km threshold is more than twice that.
          </p>
          <p class="text-muted-foreground">
            The one-day grace has the same origin. One archived task has three pilots (of
            eighty) whose recorders were stamped a day late; all three flew the task and were
            scored normally, one finishing fourth, in goal. Treating a one-day offset as
            disqualifying would have zeroed a podium flight.
          </p>
          <p class="text-muted-foreground">
            Over all <strong class="text-foreground">5,112</strong> tracks GlideComp holds, the
            checks withhold exactly one: a New Zealand flight submitted to a Victorian task ten
            days later.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>

      {/* Disclosure */}
      <section id="disclosure" class="mb-14">
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold mb-4">Nothing Happens Silently</h2>
        <div class="space-y-4 text-sm leading-relaxed">
          <p class="text-muted-foreground">
            When a check fires, the pilot's score page names it, shows the numbers behind it,
            and says what to do next. A withheld track is labelled in the results table too, so
            a zero never looks like a bug.
          </p>
          <p class="text-muted-foreground">
            Same rule as
            <a href="/scoring/data-cleaning" class="underline hover:text-foreground">track data cleaning</a>:
            anything the system did on the way to a score must be visible.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>
    </div>
  </div>
</Base>
